(Title) Tj
T*
(Adding synchronous therapeutic contact to a smoking-cessation app: results of a randomized) Tj
T*
(effectiveness trial comparing app-only versus app-plus-cognitive-behavioral-support models) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Remotely-delivered smoking cessation interventions expand access to evidence-based) Tj
T*
(treatment, but the incremental value of human therapeutic engagement over autonomous app-) Tj
T*
(based self-management remains uncertain. A randomized three-arm trial enrolled 420 adults) Tj
T*
(\(mean age ~48 years\) actively seeking smoking abstinence support. Participants were) Tj
T*
(randomly assigned to eight weeks of: \(1\) app-only self-guided use \(n=140\); \(2\) app) Tj
T*
(combined with eight weekly group cognitive-behavioral therapy sessions via secure video) Tj
T*
(\(n=140\); or \(3\) app combined with four biweekly group sessions \(n=140\). Primary outcome) Tj
T*
(was carbon-monoxide-validated 7-day point-prevalence smoking abstinence at week eight. The) Tj
T*
(app-only group achieved 18% validated abstinence at treatment completion. The high-) Tj
T*
(intensity contact arm \(eight weekly sessions\) achieved 36% validated abstinence, while the) Tj
T*
(low-intensity arm \(four biweekly sessions\) achieved 28% abstinence. Both therapeutic-) Tj
T*
(contact arms significantly outperformed the app-only condition. At 12-month follow-up, the) Tj
T*
(high-intensity group maintained substantially higher rates of sustained abstinence) Tj
T*
(compared with other study arms. These findings demonstrate that supplementing digital) Tj
T*
(smoking-cessation tools with regular human therapeutic guidance substantially improves) Tj
T*
(both short-term and long-term abstinence rates.) Tj
